I communicated with Eric offlist.  The answer to his question is that it
is not valid to sum limits of confidence intervals.  The limits for a
sum is not the sum of the limits!  In his case he used a common
detection function which meant his stratum-specific estimates were
positively correlated which resulted in limits for the sum that were
greater than the sum of the limits.  The correct way to construct
confidence intervals for a sum is to construct the variance for the sum
and then compute the limits from that variance.  The variance of a sum
of independent variables is the sum of the variances but the limits for
the sum will be less than the sum of the limits.  Do not add confidence
limits and expect that it is meaningful!  --jeff

Eric Stolen wrote:

> > Dear Group;
> > I have a question about calculating the variance of pooled density estimates

> > across strata, when components are estimated at different levels.  Often,

> > when pooling density or abundance estimates across strata, it is necessary to

> > compute the variance of the combined estimate by taking into account that

> > some of the components of the density estimators are shared between the

> > strata (e.g., f(0) ).  However, this results in confidence intervals for the
> > combined density or abundance that differ from the naive approach of simply
> > summing the lower and upper confidence limits for the individual strata (for

> > example, as output in Distance).  Can someone help me understand why the

> > naive estimates sum to the wrong numbers when combined, but are correct

